{
	title: 'Terminal client for a remote host: ./run -r <host>',
	spec: `\`./run -r <host>\` runs the terminal client against a remote hal2 host (such as example.com) over its web endpoint instead of this home's host; \`./run -r\` alone reuses the last host. <host> is a hostname with an optional port, or an http(s) origin; a bare name means https, except localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1], which mean http; a path, query or credentials are refused. The process never becomes host and never takes this home's host lock: it connects only to the remote one.

Login: the terminal logs in like a browser (task 8a): it asks for a one-time code on stdin (the user gets one from /auth or \`./run auth\` on the remote), POSTs it to /login and keeps the session token from the cookie. Host and token are remembered in state/remote.ason (mode 0600; last host and a token per host), so the next start logs in without a code. A remembered token the host refuses (GET /login 401) is forgotten and a new code is asked for; a wrong code is asked again, other failures (unreachable, 429) end the process with the reason. The token is never printed or logged.

Connection: one WebSocket at /ws carrying the token as the hal cookie and an Origin naming the remote, through the common connection core (src/common/connection.ts) with the same transport as the browser (ASON per message, reconnect with backoff, pending commands resent), so everything the local terminal does works remotely: tabs, prompts, drafts, uploads, questions, web links. Losing the host shows the usual reconnecting notice and retries forever. A socket closed with 4001 (/auth revoke), or a refused socket whose token GET /login then rejects, forgets the token and quits with a message saying to run \`./run -r\` again.

The client starts on the tab it left (a Ctrl-R restart keeps the -r arguments through ./run) or else the remote's first tab: this machine's cwd means nothing there. Local drafts of remote sessions are kept apart from this home's, under state/remote/<host>/drafts. The status row names the remote host (example.com) where a local client shows host or peer. Ctrl-C quits the client without pausing anything on the remote; Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-R work as always. Old Hal's version for reference: artifacts main.ts (remoteHost branch), remote-auth.ts, web-connection.ts. Tests: a code logs in and the token is remembered, a wrong code is refused, a revoked token is detected, and a client over the WebSocket gets the remote's tabs and its command is carried out.`,
	status: 'done',
	needs: [
		'8a',
		'rw'
	],
	notes: [
		"The browser's WebSocket transport moved from src/web/link.ts to src/common/ws-link.ts (object wsLink) so the terminal shares it; its reload() callback is the terminal's logout. Bun's WebSocket takes { headers } (Cookie and Origin), which host/web.ts checks exactly as for a page, so the host needed no change.",
		"Login flow and transport live in src/client/remote.ts; main.ts keeps state/remote.ason through liveFiles (client code may not import host) and runs main.initTerminal(origin), skipping host init, version checks and the Ctrl-C pause (terminal.onQuit stays a no-op). The code is read with Bun's prompt() before raw mode.",
		"The view derivation (view, status, activity, why, blinks) moved out of client/app.ts into client/app-view.ts (appView), which also holds state.remote, the host name the status row shows; app.ts keeps state, events, keys and painting (show, beat)."
	]
}
